WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.904 --> 00:00:12.202
Okay, hi everyone.

00:00:12.202 --> 00:00:15.677
We're going to talk about
public relations today.

00:00:15.677 --> 00:00:16.885
I'm Sharon.

00:00:16.885 --> 00:00:20.818
I actually work at Y Combinator, and
I work on our continuity team which is

00:00:20.818 --> 00:00:25.400
the team that does growth stage investing,
so Series B, Series C investing.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.598
And also trying to create programs and

00:00:27.598 --> 00:00:32.144
give advice to founders as they enter
into a different phase of their startup.

00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:35.014
Right after they've built a great product
they then have to go build a company.

00:00:35.014 --> 00:00:38.373
And make it a really great company so
that's what we try to do,

00:00:38.373 --> 00:00:40.970
we'll try to help with
on the continuity team.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.698
Before I was at YC, I was the Chief
Marketing Officer at GreenDot which is

00:00:44.698 --> 00:00:47.510
a fin tech company that makes
pre-paid debit cards and

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.155
checking accounts that
don't let you overdraft.

00:00:50.155 --> 00:00:52.631
They're really targeting
under banked population.

00:00:52.631 --> 00:00:54.200
It's a public company.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.447
And I got there by being acquired,
I was at Looped which is a start-up,

00:00:57.447 --> 00:00:59.136
it was actually Sam wass start-up.

00:00:59.136 --> 00:01:04.290
So I worked with Sam there for two years
and before that I was at PR agencies.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:09.376
So I was at two different PR agencies,
probably had a total of 20 clients over

00:01:09.376 --> 00:01:14.397
that period of times, ranging from
start-ups to big companies like Intel.

00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:17.216
So I've done a lot of kind
of on the ground PR work and

00:01:17.216 --> 00:01:20.110
also have thought about it
from a strategic level.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:25.640
And also get to talk to founders from YC
about startups, or about PR and reckoning.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.090
So that's why I'm here talking to you.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.010
And the tough thing is even though

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:36.140
this is going to be about PR you're not
going to escape the standard waxy advice.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.970
Which is while PR might be interesting and

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.590
something that could help in some ways for
your business.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.169
Your number one job as a founder
is to focus on your product and

00:01:45.169 --> 00:01:46.747
that's your not going to escape that.

00:01:46.747 --> 00:01:50.103
So even if PR is interesting
that's the key, the key goal.

00:01:50.103 --> 00:01:53.677
And I bring it up because without
product innovation PR is not going to be

00:01:53.677 --> 00:01:54.640
an option for you.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:58.368
The startup curve is the one that Paul
Graham drew way back it probably looks

00:01:58.368 --> 00:01:58.968
familiar.

00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:03.723
But the fact is you could get some great
article written about your product.

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:07.654
But if you get people in there and they're
disenchanted by it or not impressed,

00:02:07.654 --> 00:02:09.340
they're not going to come back.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:14.550
So your not going to have something that's
ongoing that you can really depend on.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.840
It's not a customer acquisition channel.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:21.637
So if press motivates you, if being on the
cover of Wired is something that you think

00:02:21.637 --> 00:02:23.292
would be cool that's cool.

00:02:23.292 --> 00:02:29.143
And have that motivate you, and create
a product that press cannot ignore, right.

00:02:29.143 --> 00:02:33.348
That's how you get great PR results,
is creating an amazing product.

00:02:33.348 --> 00:02:35.390
So that's the first thing you can do.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:36.030
Now that's said.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:40.034
[LAUGH] You know it's your first job but
there are some things,

00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:43.347
I am going to actually give
you some tangible tools and

00:02:43.347 --> 00:02:46.790
steps that you can take
to start a PR program.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:50.806
My first tip though for
early stage founders is don't go and

00:02:50.806 --> 00:02:52.904
hire a PR agency or a PR person.

00:02:52.904 --> 00:02:58.227
I really believe for early stage
founders that Founder-Led PR is simply

00:02:58.227 --> 00:03:04.260
the only way to go for a lot of reasons,
but mainly I think it's actually special.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.324
You know reporters like
hearing from founders,

00:03:06.324 --> 00:03:08.044
that's the end spokesperson for them.

00:03:08.044 --> 00:03:12.247
So there's not someone going in between
there's someone who they genuinely can

00:03:12.247 --> 00:03:16.280
learn from and gather information
from knocking right on their door.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.028
I guess usually in this forum,
[LAUGH] an email.

00:03:19.028 --> 00:03:23.320
But you're getting exposure to someone who
could actually be a spokesperson for you.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:27.390
So it's special and it's something that's
kind of unique to the vibe that you can

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.870
pull off when you're at an early stage.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.060
And the other side is you
have something to offer.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:37.633
You may pitch them reporter today that
news may not fit into their coverage for

00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:39.356
the day or for the week.

00:03:39.356 --> 00:03:42.431
But in a month something really
relevant to that area could happen and

00:03:42.431 --> 00:03:43.920
they'll call you.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.550
Because you're a subject matter expert,
you know your domain,

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:47.890
you know your business.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:51.470
And that helps them craft the story that
helps them give background of the story.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:55.285
So I would kind of think about it as not
kind of a switch you can flip from the PR

00:03:55.285 --> 00:03:58.878
perspective but really you're
trying to establish a relationship.

00:03:58.878 --> 00:04:02.233
And trying to just get ball rolling,
getting your company name out there,

00:04:02.233 --> 00:04:03.359
your product out there.

00:04:03.359 --> 00:04:08.364
And just establishing yourself
is a credible spokesperson.

00:04:08.364 --> 00:04:12.362
So this is the next question I always
get when I say that first speech.

00:04:12.362 --> 00:04:15.281
So I just really want to take a second
to just knock down these net.

00:04:15.281 --> 00:04:19.277
And I even sent out some questions to
a lot of folks in startup school and

00:04:19.277 --> 00:04:21.580
I got some of these questions.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:25.250
So questions back about PR agencies.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:28.660
So first reason why people
might think that they really,

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.580
really need the PR person to come
in right away and help them.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.440
That they might help them define
the business and de-jargonize it.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.260
And yes, communications folks
can be really good at that.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:44.230
But you're kind of depriving yourself of
a process that's going to be really useful

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:46.640
beyond media relations.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.310
Being able to define your
business in a clear and

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.960
concise way is going to pay back
in every aspect of your business.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.162
When you're talking to
a potential investor,

00:04:54.162 --> 00:04:56.315
when you're talking to
a potential partner.

00:04:56.315 --> 00:04:59.386
When you're trying to hire
your first few engineers

00:04:59.386 --> 00:05:03.936
you need to be able to clearly pitch them
on what makes your business special.

00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:05.850
And why they should listen to you.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.731
So don't deprive yourself of that process.

00:05:08.731 --> 00:05:12.300
They come up with story angles so
they're paying attention to the news.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:16.840
Well you're a better spokesperson and
founder if you follow the news.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:19.180
You need to understand where
your business sits in the news.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.960
I'm not saying you should spend five hours
a day of poring over every news site but

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:24.050
you should have a good pulse.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:26.290
You know couple times a day checking and

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:28.480
look at the stories that
are relevant to your business and

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.540
just more broadly understand where
your business fits in the world.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:33.320
The third reason and

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:37.150
the most common reason I get is
that they know the reporters.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:38.318
They hold these relationships.

00:05:38.318 --> 00:05:39.774
They're the ones that can make it happen.

00:05:39.774 --> 00:05:43.820
And I'll tell you no
credible reporter would ever

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:49.210
write a story because they like
went to drinks with someone.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:51.508
They'll write a story because
it's a good story, right.

00:05:51.508 --> 00:05:55.710
So a story always trumps a relationship so
focus on that.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.780
Focus on a product that has a story,

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:03.240
focus on a product that has traction which
is a story and that's what can pull.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.550
That's what can start creating
those relationships for you.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.905
It's not a magic kind of
key that you can turn.

00:06:09.905 --> 00:06:11.760
They field media inquiries, right.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.210
So they'll be able to respond
to press in a timely manner.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.896
It's a pretty high-class problem to have.

00:06:16.896 --> 00:06:19.934
If you're getting tons of media
inquiries that you can barely,

00:06:19.934 --> 00:06:23.941
that you're drowning in them you probably
have some pretty good product traction.

00:06:23.941 --> 00:06:28.764
And maybe you're having a blazing
success and then you have permission to

00:06:28.764 --> 00:06:33.523
hire a strategic communications
partner who can help you through that.

00:06:33.523 --> 00:06:36.320
And lastly they're good
at sending invoices.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.490
Not to minimize that to a PR agency or
a PR professional but

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.545
it's essentially overhead in a lot of ways
because there's no guaranteed results.

00:06:45.545 --> 00:06:48.280
It could be an agency can come in.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:51.910
They don't know your business,
they have to learn your business.

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:54.650
So probably about a month out and
then they start pitching.

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:55.600
It might be two weeks out,

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.650
three weeks out, two months before
you even get a story in the news.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:04.450
And it's even then that's not
guarantee to drive specific business

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:08.490
school because it's not an engaged
measurable customer acquisition tactic.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:12.560
So get to a place where you have
a really good team that can

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:15.610
build that great product engineering team,
your product team, yourself.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:18.820
And that where you
should kind of focus on.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:24.052
So hopefully I've convinced you but
I do think that there are some simple

00:07:24.052 --> 00:07:28.874
things the founders can do to chip
away at the PR goals themselves.

00:07:28.874 --> 00:07:32.992
And eventually once you are a success
you should hire a PR firm,

00:07:32.992 --> 00:07:35.490
they're cool, I used to work in one.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:36.570
Five steps.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.780
And these are really based
on my experience but

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:42.680
I'm also leaving some common YC advice
that batches receive when they come

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:46.590
through YC specifically this one,
step 1, who are you?

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:53.755
So this long list is a checklist of
a whiteboarding session at Y Combinator.

00:07:53.755 --> 00:07:58.333
So companies that come through YC will get
this great presentation by Kat Manalac

00:07:58.333 --> 00:08:00.160
who's a partner at YC.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.600
And then they'll meet with
their group partners,

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:04.840
and go through each of these questions.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:10.300
And this is something that I implore
you to take time and think about.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:15.290
So invite a family member or a friend in,
someone who's not steeped in

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:19.630
your world who can actually kind of
give you some real advice on whether or

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:24.760
not your description is jargon or makes
sense to the common man in Anytown, USA.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:26.120
But most importantly,
what does your company do?

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:30.040
We will talk about that more,
being able to describe your customers.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.590
So how does your product
live through your customer.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:33.730
Through the lens of their customer.

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:37.260
People like stories about people so
this is a great one for PR.

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:40.620
What problem are you solving?

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.390
How are you measuring, how you are going?

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:43.870
And how are you goiing?

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:47.380
Who your competitors, and
why are you better than your competitors?

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:48.460
What are your plans for next year?

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:52.830
And I think one of the more important
ones for PR as well is why you?

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:54.730
Why did you start this company?

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:58.310
Why are you uniquely qualified to
bring this product to the world?

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:03.800
And that can range from stories of
something you over came that cause

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:08.190
you to explore this field and to conquer
this technology and this innovation.

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.830
Or it could be that you came from
a company that has done this kind of and

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:13.885
you're, you broke off and
are doing it better.

00:09:13.885 --> 00:09:17.300
So the founder stories are really
great to establish early

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:19.250
on cause sometimes we could
forget that about ourselves,

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:21.490
right that we bring something
special to the table.

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:26.860
So to dive a little bit deeper on,
what does your company do?

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.430
The biggest advice is to lead
with the what, not the why.

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:31.720
And what I mean by that,

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:34.010
is even though we've established
that you're all co-founders.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:37.140
And that you're uniquely
qualified to start your business.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:40.980
That's kind of a long story to get,
all at once.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:44.970
So instead of some like big paragraph
where I have to really try to navigate to

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:50.700
understand what your company actually is,
it's a lot easier for

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:56.800
you to say to me, well, Wright Electric is
Boeing for electric airplanes, I get that.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.103
You're making them, they're electric,
tell me more, right I want to lean in,

00:10:01.103 --> 00:10:02.630
I want to hear more.

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.850
Actual company from the last
batch of YC by the way.

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:12.260
Dropbox, is synchronizes your file
across your and your team's computers.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:15.230
That's easy to understand,
it might seem silly now, but

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:19.390
this is actually their description under
YC application years and years ago.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:28.010
So the overall summary here is, make it
conversational, which means no jargon.

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:31.937
No preamble, just get right to it and
above all, make it concise.

00:10:35.558 --> 00:10:39.978
So step 2, we're going to define
your goals and your audience.

00:10:39.978 --> 00:10:44.100
And this one,
it's kind of a forced principles type

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:48.570
process because PR actually isn't a goal.

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:52.040
So, saying my goal is to get a lot of
press, it actually isn't your goal.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:56.800
If you really, really step back, you're
actually striving toward a business goal.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.822
So you want to maybe
recruit the best talent,

00:10:58.822 --> 00:11:03.710
you want to finance your growth,
drive trial, land strategic partners.

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:05.790
You have things that you're
trying to do with your business.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:10.730
So you want to ask yourself,
this is the business thing I want to do.

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:12.320
Is there a strategic,

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.305
is there an audience that I can maybe
influence to help me get there?

00:11:15.305 --> 00:11:19.770
Press aren't the audience,
[LAUGH] they have readers though.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:24.751
So press might be the window
to your audience, so talking,

00:11:24.751 --> 00:11:28.870
getting a story placed or
working with press to get some story place

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:31.530
could be a way to reach
this priority audience.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:35.250
So if your priority or
your business goal is to finance growth

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:39.290
your audience might be investors, right?

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:43.360
So that's kind of how you need to break
it down, and really try to prioritize it,

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:44.760
because if everything's a priority,

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:47.670
then nothing's a priority,
so really to focus in.

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:50.440
If your goal of press is investors,

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:55.830
it's actually going to influence
the reporters that you try to talk to,

00:11:55.830 --> 00:12:00.550
and the publications that you try to get
in and so you want to ask to yourself.

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:04.160
The other, obviously, piece of YC advice,
that I'm sure you're all already doing

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.580
is building your product and talking to
customers and repeat, repeat, repeat.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:13.420
So if you're talking to your customers
anyway, maybe ask them what they read.

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:18.967
That's a good way to kind of get to
what outlets you might want to consider.

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:25.450
So which brings us to our next step,
step 3, reading.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:26.880
Which might seem common sense but, so

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:29.250
if you all ready read the news,
probably you all ready do.

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:33.020
It's just maybe putting a little more
structure around how you look at the news.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:37.060
So if you read a really compelling article
that you feel like hit some of the notes

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:41.420
that you, as a founder, represent or
that your product speaks to.

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:43.170
Then look at who wrote it, right?

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:46.750
That person maybe will interested in
a story about you and your company.

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:51.763
So the big tips here, as you kind of
prepare for, and this is also, I get a lot

00:12:51.763 --> 00:12:55.903
of questions from technical founders on
like how to bring efficiency to this.

00:12:55.903 --> 00:13:00.670
And the real answer is,
there's not tons of efficiency in this.

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:02.548
This is about relationship-building,

00:13:02.548 --> 00:13:04.520
there's not something
that can automate it.

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:08.990
But here's some tools,
a Google spreadsheet, so

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:12.290
that's the biggest tool I think
that people might use for PR.

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:14.390
So just kind of organizing your thoughts.

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:18.780
You read a very article, grab the link,
note who wrote it and write a little bit

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:23.310
to yourself, about what was relevant and
how you think your story might

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:26.650
play with that same reporter, given their
interest and given their coverage area.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:29.250
Think about what angle
might interest them,

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:31.340
which we'll talk a little bit more about.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:34.430
And then think about connections to them.

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:38.240
So we talked about how PR agencies
aren't vital for relationships.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:42.180
But it is nice to get a warm
introduction to a reporter.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:46.990
So, if you see that that reporter
has written about someone you know,

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:48.370
or an investor might know them.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:51.115
So maybe another founder
has appeared in the story?

00:13:51.115 --> 00:13:54.180
Seem might reach out to that founder,
draft an email up for them and

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:56.753
say hey would you mind
introducing me to this reporter,

00:13:56.753 --> 00:13:58.963
I think they might be
interested in my product.

00:13:58.963 --> 00:14:05.490
Right, so that's the connection
with the system column.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:10.165
On angle, I think I kind of wanted to
specifically point out business press,

00:14:10.165 --> 00:14:14.795
because for an early stage startup, it's
not always a relevant target for people.

00:14:14.795 --> 00:14:17.895
I'm going to do an audience question now.

00:14:17.895 --> 00:14:22.353
What would you guess is the most common
topic in covered in business outlets?

00:14:22.353 --> 00:14:26.106
&gt;&gt; Funding?

00:14:26.106 --> 00:14:29.019
&gt;&gt; Maybe, yes for start ups.

00:14:29.019 --> 00:14:31.140
Yes, so the most top topic is business.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:36.330
You have to have something that ties like
market moving, commerce, the economy.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:39.820
So funding is a good example for
where start up might get into this.

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:43.830
Another example where star up might be
able to get an a story in the business

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:47.410
press would be if you are really
getting a lot attraction and

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:50.250
like upsetting the stream
of someone else's business.

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:53.150
So you're taking advertising
dollars away from Facebook.

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:58.150
Or you have an app that can hail a car.

00:14:58.150 --> 00:15:01.230
And it's maybe disrupting a taxi business.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:04.110
These are kind of things that will get you
into this press even before you get big.

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:06.550
Because you're showing
signs of disruption,

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:08.785
you're showing signs that there's
something to look at here.

00:15:08.785 --> 00:15:13.830
So one to kind of cover that one, because
it's a common question, the other element.

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:15.770
So as you start reaching out to reporters,
and

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:18.630
I'll give you some example of
pictures that have actually gone out.

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:23.360
And just a reminder that everything
you say is on the record.

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.340
The reporters job is to get the story and

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:30.160
they just tell kind of Gathering
all the information you give them.

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:35.320
I mean, if you share metrics of your
business, they may wrote about them.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:39.070
So it's the common advice we give YC
companies before demo day too, right?

00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:41.760
When they're really excited to share
everything about their business, and

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:42.570
they share all their metrics.

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:44.420
And they might be
surprised the next day when

00:15:45.470 --> 00:15:47.340
an article talks about
the metrics of their business.

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:49.710
So just,
it might seem like common sense advice.

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:51.440
But it's really easy to
forget in the moments.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:54.450
So just keep in mind,
be open, be transparent.

00:15:55.470 --> 00:16:00.600
Share honestly, but if you don't know your
metrics in the news, don't share them.

00:16:03.498 --> 00:16:04.890
Okay.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:08.200
So what to say and timed outreach.

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:12.591
So going to start reaching out you have
already looked at your kind of year ahead

00:16:12.591 --> 00:16:15.264
and thought about,
what news your might have?

00:16:15.264 --> 00:16:18.500
Maybe it's your actual company launch,
maybe it's a product launch.

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:21.240
Maybe you have fund raising.

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:22.550
Maybe you got some investment.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:24.300
You might hit some milestone.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:26.540
Maybe you have some
statistics that are cool.

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:29.058
These are all examples
of company fueled news.

00:16:29.058 --> 00:16:32.730
The other type of news
is trend based news.

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.940
How your company fits in a bigger trend?

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:40.840
Things like a round-up
of new finance apps.

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:42.702
Maybe you're going to be one
of those apps, that's name.

00:16:42.702 --> 00:16:47.339
I usually say that journals will usually
only name three, they're not going to

00:16:47.339 --> 00:16:52.992
spend their day listing an exhaustive list
that have all the 15, 20, 30 offset exist.

00:16:52.992 --> 00:16:57.856
So to the degree that you have engaged
with the reporter in a meaningful way

00:16:57.856 --> 00:17:01.629
it maybe they don't write
about your new story one week.

00:17:01.629 --> 00:17:03.735
Or maybe three weeks from now
they're going to do a round up and

00:17:03.735 --> 00:17:04.374
you'll be in it.

00:17:04.374 --> 00:17:07.962
So there are some things where
just the relationship and

00:17:07.962 --> 00:17:11.487
just reaching out is good and
can kind of pay back later.

00:17:11.487 --> 00:17:13.880
Also politics and entertainment.

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:17.442
When that kind of news happens sometimes
there's an opportunity to jump on and

00:17:17.442 --> 00:17:18.603
be included in some way.

00:17:18.603 --> 00:17:20.653
And also contextualizing
other company announcements.

00:17:20.653 --> 00:17:24.972
So if Apple has big developer news then
maybe your developer have something to say

00:17:24.972 --> 00:17:29.240
or contribute, or reaching out and
saying hey, here's what I think about it.

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:30.301
Let me know if you want to talk.

00:17:30.301 --> 00:17:33.992
That can be a way to get your
company into the conversation.

00:17:33.992 --> 00:17:37.090
So here's an actually example.

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:43.060
So simple habit within YC last batch, and
it's essentially Spotify for meditation.

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:50.272
Yunha, the founder specifically wanted
to get a coverage in indulge it.

00:17:50.272 --> 00:17:52.710
So she found this reporter, Nathan.

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:55.750
He wrote several times about
different streaming apps and

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:58.798
we're specifically interested
in New kinds of content.

00:17:58.798 --> 00:18:02.416
So he's written about Spotify,
written about YouTube TV and so

00:18:02.416 --> 00:18:04.110
she approached him.

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:05.980
She had some seed funding to announce.

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:09.070
Engadget doesn't actually write that much
about fundraising and she knew that.

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:11.590
So she reached out shared
the fundraising news.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:15.120
But also shared that they were going to be
doing a new release that had a bunch more

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:18.610
content coming out and
a few new features and he was interested.

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:20.100
He asked for more information.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:23.324
She searched from Visuals and
this is the story.

00:18:23.324 --> 00:18:24.563
This is the story [LAUGH].

00:18:24.563 --> 00:18:29.895
And so that's a great example of
something that simple habit did.

00:18:29.895 --> 00:18:33.024
So a relevant pitch to the right
reporter around a news item.

00:18:33.024 --> 00:18:35.560
Here's another example.

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:40.880
So this is not company milestone-based but
actually customer-based.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:44.815
So like we talked about showing
your product through the eyes

00:18:44.815 --> 00:18:47.207
of a customer this YC company again,

00:18:47.207 --> 00:18:52.237
Thunkable which lets anybody build
mobile apps without knowing how to code.

00:18:52.237 --> 00:18:55.456
They discovered this customer
they had in Yemen who,

00:18:55.456 --> 00:18:57.930
again without knowing how to code.

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:02.650
Developed a solar app or basically
an app that brought thousands of people

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:06.790
solar power that had lost power
due to civil war in Yemen and

00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:08.290
it's a pretty cool story, right?

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:11.020
So they drafted up a pitch.

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:13.020
They knew someone who knew the reporter.

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:17.699
The founder knew someone who knew
the reporter and drafted up an email for

00:19:17.699 --> 00:19:21.480
that person to then send to
a reporter of that's company.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:26.850
So this is the email asking if they can
intro giving a little bit of context,

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:29.010
giving that really short
business description.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:33.989
And the reporter was interested
this is the story of past company.

00:19:37.477 --> 00:19:40.451
And here's the example of writing the news
wave, we talked about politics and

00:19:40.451 --> 00:19:41.135
entertainment.

00:19:41.135 --> 00:19:43.195
Approximately being something to jump on.

00:19:43.195 --> 00:19:45.890
It's not everyday that
this is an opportunity so

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:47.740
this is not your common example.

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:51.360
But we all might remember
last October there was some

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:55.180
tapes that were leaked of Donald Trump
saying lewd things on a bus.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:20:00.410
And it was to put it mildly major news
in the US and probably around the world.

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:03.948
And so this happened October eighth,
I think.

00:20:03.948 --> 00:20:10.350
So deepgram, another YC company their
product is basically search for speech.

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:14.040
So they can search audio files like
Google searches images, right?

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:17.520
And so they saw an opportunity
with all this talk about

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:20.470
are there more tapes is there
anything else to discover?

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:23.230
Journalists are going down
that path of inquiry and so

00:20:23.230 --> 00:20:26.630
they came in with what I would
consider kind of a light stun.

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:30.866
Which is hey, we're making our products
free for journalists until election day.

00:20:30.866 --> 00:20:32.499
Have at it search for whatever.

00:20:32.499 --> 00:20:35.080
And so they got were covered for that.

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:38.526
So they were able to quickly think
about it they put up a blog post and

00:20:38.526 --> 00:20:41.740
they jumped on it and
this is the story they got in Tech Crunch.

00:20:44.833 --> 00:20:47.080
So those are a few examples.

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:50.810
You saw a few examples of pitches and
I would say that's your number one tool.

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:53.799
You're very likely not in a place where
reporters are kind of sitting on your site

00:20:53.799 --> 00:20:54.702
refreshing your blog.

00:20:54.702 --> 00:20:58.559
Wondering what the next news is going
to be, so you need to get to them.

00:20:58.559 --> 00:21:04.450
And so blog post is helpful to link
to just to give extra information.

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:08.912
And don't attach like to not attach steps
so it's a good kind of repository for

00:21:08.912 --> 00:21:13.255
the full story to have that if someone
is interested they can click through.

00:21:13.255 --> 00:21:15.800
I get questions about press releases.

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:17.610
They're not really for
start-ups, that's the truth.

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:23.340
It's an older medium, gosh it used
to be even more intense than this.

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:26.650
I mean, there was faxes coming
into the newsroom that used

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:28.870
to be the big utility of a press release.

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:32.040
And then more recently it was a good
way to get in Google news, right?

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:37.340
It got you in search results, but
Google has wised up about that a lot.

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:38.710
So it doesn't really do that anymore.

00:21:38.710 --> 00:21:42.710
So your blog has just as much power
to get into the search results as

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:44.106
a press release does.

00:21:44.106 --> 00:21:47.385
And press releases if you're
going to put them over the wire,

00:21:47.385 --> 00:21:49.630
cost a couple thousand dollars.

00:21:49.630 --> 00:21:51.600
So it's not worth it for a startup.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:56.650
Really they're a tool for public companies
to make disclosures about market moving

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:59.420
news so
that's the main folks that'll use it.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:03.508
I guess the only other exception
would be if you are in an industry

00:22:03.508 --> 00:22:07.765
that's an older-fashioned industry
like banking or aviation.

00:22:07.765 --> 00:22:12.947
Sometimes I go link to a press release on
your site can establish credibility for

00:22:12.947 --> 00:22:14.290
partners.

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:18.834
Not press but like partners or other
people that you might want to get meetings

00:22:18.834 --> 00:22:21.180
with for lack of other press coverage.

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:22.651
So there's that.

00:22:22.651 --> 00:22:25.750
Visuals and
assets are really great to include.

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:29.283
Never an attachments because attachments
are annoying, we all know that.

00:22:29.283 --> 00:22:33.163
But just link to them like
either in your site or

00:22:33.163 --> 00:22:38.670
at the drop box link because every
story will use a visual anyway.

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:41.794
A lot of outlets want to create their
own visual for bigger feather stories.

00:22:41.794 --> 00:22:45.934
But for like a smaller news item they
might just like screenshot your website or

00:22:45.934 --> 00:22:49.670
find a picture of the founder
like speaking at some event.

00:22:49.670 --> 00:22:51.708
But maybe you don't like that picture,
maybe it's not flattering.

00:22:51.708 --> 00:22:54.060
So I always just say
provide your own visuals so

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:57.980
hopefully they'll use one of those and
you'll be happy.

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:00.540
So I don't know why I shoved
all this in one slide.

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:04.660
But the other thing I want to talk about
in terms of official announcements

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:06.626
are exclusives versus embargos.

00:23:06.626 --> 00:23:10.502
Which could be its own slide about
how to really, really, really,

00:23:10.502 --> 00:23:14.669
really pissed off a reporter so listen
closely because these are important.

00:23:14.669 --> 00:23:18.093
These are two tools
that are used in the PR

00:23:18.093 --> 00:23:22.432
world to brief a reporter
in advance of a news date.

00:23:22.432 --> 00:23:25.600
So that they can have a full story
ready to go on the day of the news.

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:30.050
You can't use them both together,
I'm going to talk about them individually.

00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:34.860
An exclusive is I'm going to talk to you,
you one reporter just me and you,

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:37.600
you're getting the full story and
that's it.

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:41.964
You're the only one I'm talking to about
this until after you post your story.

00:23:41.964 --> 00:23:43.813
And then if someone calls
me I can talk to them but

00:23:43.813 --> 00:23:45.720
they're not going to have
the story ready to go.

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:46.725
It's yours.

00:23:46.725 --> 00:23:48.740
That's an exclusive.

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:53.030
It's as serious as a monogamous relation
like that is you should not mess with

00:23:53.030 --> 00:23:53.890
an exclusive.

00:23:53.890 --> 00:23:56.800
If you promise to give someone the story,
it's theirs.

00:23:58.210 --> 00:23:59.370
Got it?
Okay.

00:23:59.370 --> 00:24:02.970
An embargo is if you have
bigger news that you think

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:07.130
multiple reporters might cover,
which is common, especially for

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:11.590
a mid-sized startup that has people
that are kind of following their news,

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:16.420
you can reach out, and say to each
of the reporters, I have some news.

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:18.370
I'd like to pre-brief you on it.

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:21.820
The news is going out on
June 15 at eight AM pacific.

00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:23.710
Would you like the news?

00:24:23.710 --> 00:24:25.230
Will you agree to embargo?

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:25.990
And they say yes.

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:27.890
There goes the saying, give me the news,

00:24:27.890 --> 00:24:31.470
if I write a story I will not publish it
until that specific time that you told me.

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:36.780
So the trick here is, you just need
make sure you're being really clear in

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:42.550
communication with everyone, because if
you send one reporter the wrong time and

00:24:42.550 --> 00:24:44.030
they like post their time earlier,

00:24:44.030 --> 00:24:48.700
then there are four reporters that are all
ready, but they were supporting your ask.

00:24:48.700 --> 00:24:54.530
So it's kind of a little weird, but these
are things that exist that are tools.

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:59.358
I would say specifically for an exclusive,
it's something we use a lot for

00:24:59.358 --> 00:25:02.640
early stage start-ups, because if
you're reaching out to a reporter and

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:04.290
you have like that, the customer story.

00:25:04.290 --> 00:25:05.930
The one from Funkable?

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:07.810
That was actually offered as an exclusive.

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:11.670
So that was just kind of an extra carrot
to say 'hey, this is a cool story,

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:14.910
and we'll give it to you, just you'.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:15.890
So the reporter knows okay,

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:19.030
I can either take my time to
actually talk to the customer.

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:21.570
And I'm not racing against
the clock to see because

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:23.340
someone else might write it before I do,
right?

00:25:23.340 --> 00:25:25.510
So it allows them to take their time.

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:27.920
Take a breath and
write the story they want.

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:29.920
And also it's nice to
not have to compete for

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:32.670
clicks on the story with other outlets.

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:36.240
So that's why it's used as a tool, but

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:39.330
just make sure to respect it and
use it carefully.

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:46.480
So last of all, listen and be a source.

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:50.590
So it's one thing to reach
out when you have news,

00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:54.110
but kind of on your day to day,
you're really just following the news and

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:57.610
possibly taking opportunities
to be a source.

00:25:57.610 --> 00:25:59.290
So these are just a couple of things.

00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:01.810
You guys know about Google Alerts and
RSS feeds.

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:04.850
One thing you might not know
about is this tool called HARO.

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:10.190
It's Help a Reporter Out and
the website is helpareporter.com.

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:12.410
And it's free, you can subscribe to it.

00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:17.260
It's kind of a lot,
you get three mondo emails every day.

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:21.480
And basically what it is is reporters
will, they're seeking a source for

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:24.800
their story, so they'll actually
submit a request for a source.

00:26:24.800 --> 00:26:25.840
So hey I'm looking for

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:30.880
an expert on molecular biology,
deadline is tomorrow at 5 PM pacific.

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:33.790
And so you see that.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:37.662
You're an expert on molecular biology,
so you can actually reply and

00:26:37.662 --> 00:26:41.230
kind of let them know about yourself and
maybe they can use you as a source.

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:46.350
So, it's a good way sometimes you can
strike it big and there's something right

00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:53.120
on the money, but it's not, I you know,
it's not a guaranteed road to coverage.

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:56.010
Also some reporters will tweet
when they need a source.

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:59.230
They'll say hey I'm looking for
someone who's an expert on this.

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:00.110
So that's another way.

00:27:00.110 --> 00:27:03.320
Also most, a lot of reporters
tweet their own coverage.

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:07.220
So again if you're reading a reporter's
coverage and kind of understanding what

00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:10.120
motivates them to write or
what types of stories are cool for them.

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:11.742
Twitter is a great place
to hang out as well.

00:27:16.149 --> 00:27:20.457
A couple quick examples of being a good
source I always give, I mean, the one I

00:27:20.457 --> 00:27:24.700
always talk about is Sam Waldman having
worked with him he just wanted to help

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:29.273
out, because he was just interested in the
story and so this is kind of the true test

00:27:29.273 --> 00:27:33.430
of the founder who gets PR from me is
someone who just thinks story is cool.

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:38.440
Someone who actually wants reporters to
have the tools to put out a great story.

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:41.990
So he would reach out even if something
wasn't within the realm of loop like if

00:27:41.990 --> 00:27:44.260
there was a story about
augmentative reality or something,

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:47.610
he would ping someone and say hey I'm
really interested in augmentative reality.

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:48.930
I know a lot about it and

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:52.990
then they would call him even though
there wasn't a specific loop tie-in.

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:55.300
That was a way that he was there for
them so

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:58.040
when we did have news they at
least looked at the email.

00:27:58.040 --> 00:28:01.750
You know, so it helped just
kind of get that credibility,

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:04.400
because they knew he was interested
in them getting a good story.

00:28:05.440 --> 00:28:09.640
Two recent examples from penny,
another YC company in a recent batch.

00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:16.750
This New York Times example was Penny
saw this comment that Jamie Diamond,

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:21.240
who's the CEO of Chase,
made about innovative

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:26.225
bank start-ups not holding a handle
to the offerings that Chase has.

00:28:26.225 --> 00:28:29.455
For their own customers, and it's kind of
crapping on innovative banking start ups.

00:28:29.455 --> 00:28:33.395
And it wasn't widely covered, like it
was only covered in a couple places,

00:28:33.395 --> 00:28:36.215
I saw it in Forbes, and I noticed
the New York Times hadn't covered it.

00:28:36.215 --> 00:28:40.635
And there was a specific reporter who they
had been continuing to read his coverage,

00:28:40.635 --> 00:28:42.565
Ron of the New York Times, Ron Lieber.

00:28:42.565 --> 00:28:45.645
And they felt like Ron would be interested
in this story, yet he hadn't covered it.

00:28:45.645 --> 00:28:46.805
So they reached out to him,

00:28:46.805 --> 00:28:50.890
said hey, have you seen this news,
what Jamie Diamond said?

00:28:50.890 --> 00:28:51.870
Here's what we think about it.

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:54.190
We think that innovative
starts do have a place.

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:56.910
And we have this perspective and
I'm an expert in this.

00:28:56.910 --> 00:28:59.580
And so he was able to jump on
the phone with a reporter,

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:01.500
give him context from what he thought.

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:05.670
And he was in this story in the second
paragraph and quoted twice.

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:08.970
So this story that he wouldn't
have been in probably, and

00:29:08.970 --> 00:29:12.650
if the reporter had picked up on his own,
probably wouldn't have been in it.

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:15.810
But he was in it because he was able
to guide the reporter through it or

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:19.530
at least help him get to a place where
where he wanted to write about it.

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:24.240
And the other thing that Penny worked
on Is, they had talked to a Bloomberg

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:28.010
reporter about chatbots, because
she had written a story about that.

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:31.660
And they kind of wanted to
give her some more experience.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:33.740
I guess I didn't really
say what Penny did.

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:35.200
They're a personal finance app, so

00:29:35.200 --> 00:29:37.570
it's like a personal finance coach
in your pocket, and it's a bot.

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:41.200
So they were talking to her about bots.

00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:43.970
And she mentioned them, and
there were two stories she was working on.

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:47.890
One about equity, grants, and
one about diversity hiring.

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:50.620
And it was just kind of conversation that

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:52.950
they realize they had a friend who
knew a lot about equity grants.

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:55.080
They connected her to their friend and

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:56.730
that was just kind of
a nice gesture they did.

00:29:56.730 --> 00:30:01.150
And then when they were starting to
look for their first engineering hire,

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:06.570
these two male founders with an app called
Penny and and a female bud wanted to

00:30:06.570 --> 00:30:10.800
hire a female engineer to help to balance
out their company from the beginning.

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:13.710
So they reached out to her and
said, hey, just interestingly,

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:16.310
I know you were interested in this,
we are actually doing this now.

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:19.090
So anyway, hope you're having a good day,
that's what we're doing.

00:30:19.090 --> 00:30:20.640
So she got back with them and said, hey,

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:22.710
I want to profile you through
the whole hiring process.

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:26.730
So she shadowed them to this in-depth
podcast on their hiring process.

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:31.120
So, this wasn't the story that they dreamt
of in Bloomberg probably, like a story of

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:35.760
the hiring process versus their business,
but is was a great way to develop this

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:38.780
relationship with this reporter, the piece
ended up turning out really great.

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:44.640
They did end up making a hire, so this to
me is just a good example of a founder

00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:50.060
that gets a good story and is like, will
invest in just kind of making that happen.

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:56.880
So this is kind of it, I think some,
the other tools that you have

00:30:56.880 --> 00:31:02.200
are responding to articles, so if there's
an article that you might even disagree

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:06.030
with, or have a different point of view,
that's another opportunity to reach out.

00:31:06.030 --> 00:31:08.840
So it's not always just news-driven or
trend-driven.

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:10.830
It could just be, hey, you wrote this.

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:12.250
I don't know if you ever
thought about it this way,

00:31:12.250 --> 00:31:16.960
but if you ever want to explore this side,
let me know, I'm here.

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:21.510
And then also, the media's changing.

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:25.580
Getting an article written about
you isn't the only way for

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:28.440
someone to start seeing you
as an authority, right?

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:32.130
So, there are small things you can
do like commenting on an article,

00:31:32.130 --> 00:31:35.720
not self-promotional,
not plugging your business in any big way,

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:38.530
just as an interested
person in this topic.

00:31:38.530 --> 00:31:41.420
Commenting on our article on Reddit,
Quora,

00:31:41.420 --> 00:31:45.640
and even creating your own
content on your blog or a medium.

00:31:45.640 --> 00:31:49.090
These are good ways to start just kind
of dipping your toe in establishing your

00:31:49.090 --> 00:31:51.420
credibility in the field
that you operate in.

00:31:51.420 --> 00:31:58.010
So those are some small things you can do,
and questions?

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:05.310
And actually, as a surprise, we have
Steven Levy here who is at Back Channel.

00:32:05.310 --> 00:32:06.410
Come on up Steven.

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:11.050
And formerly at Wired and
Newsweek way back.

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:12.015
And he is surprised, so

00:32:12.015 --> 00:32:14.200
this is a journalist you can
ask questions to as well.

00:32:14.200 --> 00:32:18.160
So you can either ask me or the person who
is on the other line of these questions.

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:21.222
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and actually this was a super
clueful presentation, I have to say,-

00:32:21.222 --> 00:32:21.806
&gt;&gt; Well thank you, Stephen.

00:32:21.806 --> 00:32:22.392
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:32:22.392 --> 00:32:26.500
And you don't hear that from
all people in the PR district.

00:32:26.500 --> 00:32:28.040
&gt;&gt; I think that's true, yeah.

00:32:28.040 --> 00:32:28.765
&gt;&gt; So very good.

00:32:28.765 --> 00:32:31.306
&gt;&gt; We do have a question right back there.

00:32:31.306 --> 00:32:35.068
&gt;&gt; Yes, I was just trying to understand
like the advantages of like the exclusive

00:32:35.068 --> 00:32:36.350
versus non-exclusive.

00:32:36.350 --> 00:32:39.797
So I imagine that being non-exclusive
could potentially lead to more press,

00:32:39.797 --> 00:32:42.093
they can at least indicate like say,
comfortable,

00:32:42.093 --> 00:32:45.589
I think comment about it like cool, so
there is like getting a better story, but

00:32:45.589 --> 00:32:48.610
what are some of the other
advantages of doing exclusive?

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:50.388
&gt;&gt; Stephen can you repeat the question?

00:32:50.388 --> 00:32:52.562
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Repeat it, so the question is,

00:32:52.562 --> 00:32:56.201
what are the advantages of
doing exclusive versus embargo,

00:32:56.201 --> 00:33:00.931
because what your understanding was that
if you do an embargo you get more stories.

00:33:00.931 --> 00:33:01.472
You want to go off that?

00:33:01.472 --> 00:33:06.597
&gt;&gt; Sure, yeah I'm dealing with
this a lot for what I do.

00:33:06.597 --> 00:33:11.543
I much prefer exclusives,
the publication I founded and

00:33:11.543 --> 00:33:16.900
write for called Backchannel is
we don't do a lot of stories,

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:21.140
we're not a trade
publication like Techfront.

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:22.750
So we're not going to cover everything.

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:26.090
We tried to cover things that are special
and we could tell things in depth.

00:33:26.090 --> 00:33:29.014
So for us,
it's important to have an exclusive.

00:33:29.014 --> 00:33:33.182
We don't want to write the generic
story that everyone else writes there.

00:33:33.182 --> 00:33:37.032
And the other problem
with embargos is that,

00:33:37.032 --> 00:33:41.589
first of what you have has to
be important to get a lot of

00:33:41.589 --> 00:33:46.602
reporters involved in there for
a non-exclusive story.

00:33:46.602 --> 00:33:51.385
And what often happens is
that someone gets wind of it.

00:33:51.385 --> 00:33:55.575
And then someone will tweet, there is
a company that does x and there's four

00:33:55.575 --> 00:34:00.093
reporters sitting there with a story and
one of them will have an editor that says,

00:34:00.093 --> 00:34:03.389
That's it the embargo's broken,
let's just go with it.

00:34:03.389 --> 00:34:06.415
So I would say well
over half the embargos,

00:34:06.415 --> 00:34:11.306
I've ever been involved with get
broken and there's bad feelings.

00:34:11.306 --> 00:34:12.764
&gt;&gt; I would have said 25%.

00:34:12.764 --> 00:34:14.600
Half is, wow.
&gt;&gt; I say over half.

00:34:14.600 --> 00:34:17.431
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean,
you know better than I do, whoa.

00:34:17.431 --> 00:34:22.092
&gt;&gt; And my pet peeve is always if a big
publication like Business Week or

00:34:22.092 --> 00:34:26.506
the New York Times breaks
an embargo sometimes accidentally,

00:34:26.506 --> 00:34:30.519
we thought it was west coast time or
something like that.

00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:34.389
They never suffer consequences there.

00:34:34.389 --> 00:34:35.056
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Exactly.

00:34:35.056 --> 00:34:39.089
&gt;&gt; But I think I argue for,
if you have an important story to tell and

00:34:39.089 --> 00:34:43.724
particularly if it's the complex story
that can't be summed up neatly in

00:34:43.724 --> 00:34:46.712
a sentence, but
what you have is really cool and

00:34:46.712 --> 00:34:51.130
needs an explanation,
then you really want an exclusive story.

00:34:51.130 --> 00:34:54.050
A reporter who's going to
find it worthwhile

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:58.000
to spend a lot of time with you and
your company and tell a definitive story.

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:02.899
You put it on the Web,
everyone gets to read it and for example,

00:35:02.899 --> 00:35:07.057
I did a story about this
company called Viv which was,

00:35:07.057 --> 00:35:10.306
people used to do the Siri product there.

00:35:10.306 --> 00:35:13.137
I did it for Wired,
it was actually probably my

00:35:13.137 --> 00:35:18.014
last story in the magazine until this
week, actually, was a couple years ago.

00:35:18.014 --> 00:35:22.454
And they actually used that on their
website to explain themselves for

00:35:22.454 --> 00:35:23.514
a couple years.

00:35:23.514 --> 00:35:24.722
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:35:24.722 --> 00:35:30.338
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I think that's the key point
about this is if someone can go deeper,

00:35:30.338 --> 00:35:33.570
then as a business, that's a tool for you.

00:35:33.570 --> 00:35:35.556
It's like a part of
a tapestry of your company.

00:35:35.556 --> 00:35:37.990
It's kind of a top of the search results.

00:35:37.990 --> 00:35:40.550
It's something that someone
can actually take and read and

00:35:40.550 --> 00:35:42.060
fall in love with your company.

00:35:42.060 --> 00:35:44.862
Especially for recruiting,
partnerships like

00:35:44.862 --> 00:35:48.900
that story is in your email signature,
and you can really cherish it.

00:35:48.900 --> 00:35:51.557
I think there's a question
right behind you.

00:35:51.557 --> 00:35:56.135
&gt;&gt; Stephen, could you go through some of
the major different types of publications?

00:35:56.135 --> 00:36:00.123
There is TechCrunch, there is New York
Times and sort of like mainstream media,

00:36:00.123 --> 00:36:04.172
but then there's the new wave of media
companies on media and then there's wait,

00:36:04.172 --> 00:36:06.597
but why because there's
all these different types?

00:36:06.597 --> 00:36:08.820
Can you sort of go through the pros and

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:12.490
cons to the different sort of
major categories that you see?

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:19.035
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure, I think ultimately
when you're going to do your pitch

00:36:19.035 --> 00:36:23.430
,you're going to do what Sharon says and
really get yourself familiar with them.

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:28.401
But you know, the larger distinctions
is that a publication like TechCrunch,

00:36:28.401 --> 00:36:33.001
you know, considers itself almost
definitive to say anything important

00:36:33.001 --> 00:36:35.097
in the industry we're going to do.

00:36:35.097 --> 00:36:40.235
So I don't know if YC still does this,
but when I last did a dive in

00:36:40.235 --> 00:36:46.708
a batch they had almost an informal
relationship that any company's time came,

00:36:46.708 --> 00:36:53.389
you know, TechCrunch would basically run
their verbal press release, you know.

00:36:53.389 --> 00:36:55.602
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; They have a call scheduled, and

00:36:55.602 --> 00:36:57.597
someone would read it out there.

00:36:57.597 --> 00:36:59.097
I don't know what,
there are so many companies-

00:36:59.097 --> 00:37:00.764
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there are so many more companies.

00:37:00.764 --> 00:37:02.682
It's a top target for
a lot like you and me.

00:37:02.682 --> 00:37:06.026
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, because they're
going to do a lot of stuff there and

00:37:06.026 --> 00:37:08.120
they're interested in the business.

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:13.770
And other traditional publications,
again, they're not going to do as much.

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:17.500
They're going to figure out,
is this significant in a business sense?

00:37:17.500 --> 00:37:21.160
Or a technology sense in which case,
,actually you might have to

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:25.510
consider a different reporter for
one of those publications there, you know.

00:37:25.510 --> 00:37:30.566
If your company is an AI company,
you're going to talk to the person who

00:37:30.566 --> 00:37:35.899
covers AI as oppose to the retail thing,
if you're a commerce company.

00:37:35.899 --> 00:37:37.140
And a lot of the newer publications.

00:37:37.140 --> 00:37:42.150
My Publications started on Medium,
we started actually as part of Medium.

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:44.760
Again, lots of different kinds of stuff.

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:48.120
Some of them are really focused
on what startup life is like.

00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:51.610
And then in which case,
you might have something unique to offer.

00:37:51.610 --> 00:37:53.430
We do a thing in my publication,

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:59.540
because a lot of people in this community
reads called war stories, that if some,

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:03.930
when it's a really interesting story
to tell, like about what they do,

00:38:03.930 --> 00:38:08.570
typically actually, people who've already
established themselves and talked about

00:38:10.780 --> 00:38:15.130
a crucial part in the formation of
their company, but you know, even so,

00:38:15.130 --> 00:38:21.020
some of the younger companies I've seen
there, how the story's interesting enough.

00:38:21.020 --> 00:38:25.150
We did it, we did one with,
what's it, instaArt?

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:25.790
&gt;&gt; Instacart?

00:38:25.790 --> 00:38:26.380
&gt;&gt; InstaArt.

00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:30.931
The art one that does Chris Chan,
I was somehow captivated with.

00:38:30.931 --> 00:38:37.528
He was this young guy from Detroit
who wound up being a sole founder,

00:38:37.528 --> 00:38:43.314
did a few pivots and
wound up to have these people in Hong Kong

00:38:43.314 --> 00:38:48.660
create paintings based on
your photographs, right?

00:38:48.660 --> 00:38:50.681
Do you remember that company?

00:38:50.681 --> 00:38:51.556
&gt;&gt; Is the painting?

00:38:51.556 --> 00:38:53.920
&gt;&gt; Is the painting, yeah,.

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:57.515
And I followed him, he was helpful,
again, against the sewers thing.

00:38:57.515 --> 00:38:58.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:38:58.170 --> 00:38:59.490
&gt;&gt; He was helpful to me,

00:38:59.490 --> 00:39:04.560
talking about his life as a startup when
I did a story about that badge and then,

00:39:04.560 --> 00:39:08.090
later I was connected with him and
wound up writing about his company.

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:09.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's great.

00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:13.880
I think, and just to add onto
the tech crunch element too,

00:39:13.880 --> 00:39:18.760
when companies are in YC, often times
their goal after YC is to get investment,

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:21.550
which is TechCrunch tends
to be their top target.

00:39:21.550 --> 00:39:23.780
It's not every company that has
that as their top target, but

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:25.400
if you think about that,
who's your audience?

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:28.305
If your audience is investors,
maybe the outlet is TechCrunch.

00:39:30.638 --> 00:39:33.830
So, I'm just wondering about the benefits
of kind of reaching out to reporters

00:39:33.830 --> 00:39:38.130
versus generating your own
PR with media articles, or

00:39:38.130 --> 00:39:41.240
Twitter, or Facebook, or
other forms of social media.

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:45.696
When you feel like you really need to
kind of reach out to reporters to kind of

00:39:45.696 --> 00:39:48.473
generate the kind of
publicity that [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:48.473 --> 00:39:49.390
&gt;&gt; The question is,

00:39:49.390 --> 00:39:53.549
how do you weigh reaching out to reporters
against things that you can do yourself

00:39:53.549 --> 00:39:57.225
like writing media articles and
being active on social media?

00:39:57.225 --> 00:39:58.720
I'm sure you have an opinion.

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:02.500
My opinion is pretty brief,
it's that they're not mutually exclusive.

00:40:02.500 --> 00:40:06.230
I think that your story through
your voice is amazing and

00:40:06.230 --> 00:40:10.030
if you're able to start developing
content that's on point for

00:40:10.030 --> 00:40:14.630
your customers, and people who are
following your company, that's amazing.

00:40:14.630 --> 00:40:16.440
And that's helpful for SEO so

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:21.950
from a pure like business standpoint,
it's helpful but it's also really

00:40:21.950 --> 00:40:25.310
great to create a dialogue where you can
actually answer your customer's problems.

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:30.230
The other thing that's helpful is to have
a third-party validate your business

00:40:30.230 --> 00:40:32.270
by thinking it's cool right and
writing a piece on it.

00:40:35.460 --> 00:40:36.580
&gt;&gt; That's generally right, but

00:40:36.580 --> 00:40:39.280
if everything is out in the medium story-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:44.570
&gt;&gt; It's a little less delicious for
a reporter to do that.

00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:49.445
I did a story about a start-up
called Bonsai recently,

00:40:49.445 --> 00:40:54.113
and interestingly that came
from a relationship I had

00:40:54.113 --> 00:40:57.847
with a PR person who
really knows my work and

00:40:57.847 --> 00:41:03.136
knows this is a guy who likes to
do that really move the ball and

00:41:03.136 --> 00:41:10.010
this is something different there that I
think is going to meet Steven's needs.

00:41:10.010 --> 00:41:14.605
And they had those and the stuff are
medium, and I look hard at that to say,

00:41:14.605 --> 00:41:17.330
boy, did they give their story away.

00:41:17.330 --> 00:41:23.672
Even though obviously if you just put
up stuff on media, and not necessarily,

00:41:23.672 --> 00:41:29.261
it therefore would be pretty rare
to get a huge readership from that.

00:41:29.261 --> 00:41:33.974
I'd say ultimately then, it doesn't
really matter whether many people

00:41:33.974 --> 00:41:37.470
have read it but
if you got what you wanted and took off,

00:41:37.470 --> 00:41:42.810
it might make a reporter think twice to
say gee that story's been told already.

00:41:42.810 --> 00:41:46.810
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that might have blown
past the common sense point of the big

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:48.380
part of the news is that it's new.

00:41:48.380 --> 00:41:50.030
Like that someone hasn't
talked about it yet.

00:41:50.030 --> 00:41:52.730
And that's a compelling point for
reporters.

00:41:54.170 --> 00:41:56.145
Any other questions?

00:41:56.145 --> 00:41:59.772
&gt;&gt; When you talk about the balance
between sort of like crafting and

00:41:59.772 --> 00:42:04.206
scaffolding a story that you directly
give to reporters versus letting them do

00:42:04.206 --> 00:42:05.890
what they want with it.

00:42:05.890 --> 00:42:07.470
Because on one hand,
it's less work for them.

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:12.220
But on the other hand, they sort
of exercise less creativity if you

00:42:12.220 --> 00:42:15.890
just give them a narrative they can
copy and paste into their article.

00:42:15.890 --> 00:42:18.790
&gt;&gt; So the question is, and
I'm going to ask Steven to answer it,

00:42:18.790 --> 00:42:23.360
is what's the balance between
doing a lot of pre-work and

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:27.430
giving kind of like an outline to
a reporter, as you said scaffolding,

00:42:27.430 --> 00:42:31.712
versus just letting
a reporter run with it.

00:42:31.712 --> 00:42:37.330
&gt;&gt; You're going to need to focus
in order to explain what it is,

00:42:37.330 --> 00:42:41.250
but once the things get rolling,

00:42:41.250 --> 00:42:48.080
the reporter is going to want to
take on the thing there.

00:42:48.080 --> 00:42:51.000
And people, reporters,

00:42:51.000 --> 00:42:55.550
just don't like being directed,
or saying, this is our story.

00:42:55.550 --> 00:42:56.870
Don't pay attention to that.

00:42:58.250 --> 00:43:03.963
So once you open the door, we're in.

00:43:03.963 --> 00:43:08.680
I hope what you want to say is
interesting enough to keep us from

00:43:08.680 --> 00:43:10.870
what we find interesting.

00:43:10.870 --> 00:43:15.870
&gt;&gt; And I think pre-work is helpful,
at least in my luck getting responses from

00:43:15.870 --> 00:43:19.190
folks, because you have to make sure
that they get that it's a cool story.

00:43:19.190 --> 00:43:23.330
So just saying, this is my company, you're
fat, is maybe not going to be enough.

00:43:23.330 --> 00:43:25.920
You need to talk to them about the cool
thing you're doing that they might be

00:43:25.920 --> 00:43:29.960
interested in, and it also brings up
another point that there are no rewrites.

00:43:29.960 --> 00:43:33.846
[LAUGH] So a reporter gets to have
their time with your story and

00:43:33.846 --> 00:43:36.967
you don't kind of get to review a draft or
edit it,

00:43:36.967 --> 00:43:40.942
which I know seems common sense but
it is a question I get a lot.

00:43:40.942 --> 00:43:44.108
Any other questions?

00:43:44.108 --> 00:43:46.653
Okay, thank you, and thank you to
our surprise guest, Steven Levy.

00:43:46.653 --> 00:43:47.165
Thank you.

00:43:47.165 --> 00:43:49.941
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

